It seems from the title that I'm going to talk about #impostor-syndrome again.
But no, not this time.

I want to note down this recurring frustration: when I work on something truly
difficult, my competence seems insufficient to get the job done.

I’m perfectly willing to admit that I need to improve myself, but that doesn’t
solve the problem. I still have to get the thing done --- completed, finished.
Time is always limited, and I fail to break the work down into actionable steps.

Seeking external help doesn’t make much difference either. The task is buried in
a huge context, too large to explain within the time available.

It always comes to my mind: I just need a higher-order version of myself.

But that version of me doesn’t exist yet --- and waiting for it won’t meet the
deadline. Maybe the only way to become that higher-order self is to do the work
badly first, and let the discomfort shape me. Perhaps the version of me who can
handle this task will be built through the struggle itself, not before it.

So when competence does not suffice, don't wait for a level-up. Get a small
failure out fast, then iterate on the feedback.

